update public.ideas
set
  summary = 'A 30-day referral-and-dispatch trial testing whether one clinic can close essential-trip gaps through a licensed accessible transport partner—without collecting fares or promising future rides.',
  description = $description$Essential trips can fail because an existing transit schedule, vehicle, or booking process does not fit a specific appointment. Before proposing a broad ride program, Neighbor Ride Credits would test one narrower question: can a single clinic identify enough unresolved trips for one licensed accessible transport partner to serve reliably?

A proposed 30-day trial would cover one clinic, one provider, one essential-trip class, and a maximum of 20 referred rides. Volunteer driving, cash handling, open-ended eligibility, and cross-provider dispatch would remain out of scope. The clinic would validate referral need before a trip is offered, while the provider would retain responsibility for licensing, insurance, vehicle safety, accessibility, and driver screening. Records would be limited to contact, pickup, scheduling, and requested accessibility details, then deleted on a published schedule.

The trial would proceed only after written clinic and provider agreements, confirmed insurance, available accessible capacity, a complaint path, and participant consent. Continue evidence would require at least twelve qualified requests, ten completed trips, median booking confirmation within one business day, documented unmet accessibility needs, and zero preventable safety incidents. Missing demand or provider capacity would revise the model; any unresolved safeguarding or authorization failure would stop it.$description$,
  updated_at = '2026-08-10 13:25:00+00'
where id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000203'
  and slug = 'neighbor-ride-credits';

update public.ideas
set
  summary = 'Three neighborhood teach-back sessions where residents learn one practical repair skill, repeat it without assistance, and leave behind a tested one-page guide.',
  description = $description$Broad skill-sharing events can feel welcoming without showing whether anyone gained a repeatable ability. Skill Swap Saturdays would narrow its first proposed cohort to four household-repair modules where an attendee can demonstrate the result: diagnosing a lamp, patching a bicycle tube, sewing a durable repair, and replacing common non-energized hardware.

The proposed test would use four instructors, 24 seats, and three Saturdays in one accessible venue. Each instructor would prepare a safety-reviewed task, a photographed one-page guide, required tools, and an equivalent low-dexterity or low-vision path. After guided practice, participants would complete a teach-back without instructor intervention and identify any step the guide failed to explain. No participant would work on energized equipment, structural systems, gas appliances, or another person's property without written authorization.

Scheduling would wait until four instructors, likely participants, venue permission, childcare demand, translation needs, tool safety, and insurance boundaries are documented. Continue evidence would require at least sixteen attendees, twelve successful teach-backs, four accessibility-checked guides, and four participants willing to return as peer helpers. Lower completion would trigger a narrower module redesign rather than a larger event.$description$,
  updated_at = '2026-08-10 13:25:00+00'
where id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000206'
  and slug = 'skill-swap-saturdays';

update public.ideas
set
  summary = 'A three-window, 90-night corridor test using permissioned light-and-art installations to measure evening attention, dwell time, and perceived welcome.',
  description = $description$A glowing vacant window is visually appealing, but the useful question is whether a small, permissioned installation changes how people experience one block after ordinary business hours. After-Dark Storefronts would test that claim with three windows, six consented exhibitors, and a defined observation period rather than promising a permanent arts program.

Before installation, the team would secure signed owner agreements covering access, content approval, electrical load, timers, glare limits, fire egress, maintenance, insurance, and removal. Two weeks of baseline pedestrian observations would be collected during equivalent evening hours, followed by voluntary intercept surveys during the 90-night display. The test would count coarse passersby and dwell intervals only; it would not use facial recognition, device tracking, recorded conversations, or covert cameras.

The test would proceed only if all three permissions, six ready exhibits, a qualified installer, and a documented removal plan are in place. Continue evidence would require at least two windows operating for 90% of scheduled hours, a measurable increase in voluntary dwell or reported welcome, no unresolved safety complaints, and owner and exhibitor willingness to repeat. Missing permissions, weak engagement, or recurring glare and access problems would trigger revision or archive.$description$,
  updated_at = '2026-08-10 13:25:00+00'
where id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000204'
  and slug = 'after-dark-storefronts';

update public.ideas
set
  summary = 'A one-building outage drill testing whether named stewards can locate, inventory, and deploy shared power, lighting, and accessible alerts within 20 minutes.',
  description = $description$Emergency kits often become an unowned box of expired supplies intended for every possible disaster. Block-Ready Kits would start with one apartment building and one scenario: an overnight power outage that interrupts hallway lighting, resident charging, ordinary communications, and accessible alerts before outside help is needed.

A proposed pilot would define a sealed inventory, primary and backup stewards, a handoff log, quarterly inspection checklist, accessible notification method, and exact storage permissions. The kit would focus on battery lighting, charging banks, radios, printed contacts, and agreed accessibility adaptations. It would not store medication, collect medical or immigration details, perform wellness enforcement, enter homes without consent, or claim to replace utilities and emergency services.

The building would complete one tabletop exercise and one daylight drill using simulated needs. Continue evidence would require a complete inventory, two trained backup stewards, deployment within 20 minutes, successful accessible notification to participating households, and a written correction for every missed handoff. Before acquiring supplies, the proposal must validate building permission, steward coverage, resident demand, the chosen scenario, and the inventory gaps households cannot already meet.$description$,
  updated_at = '2026-08-10 13:25:00+00'
where id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000208'
  and slug = 'block-ready-kits';

update public.ideas
set
  summary = 'A two-site smoke-readiness trial lending ten room-matched HEPA cleaners and verifying checkout speed, indoor PM2.5 improvement, and filter stewardship.',
  description = $description$Cleaner-air lending works only when each unit produces enough clean air for its room, avoids harmful byproducts, reaches borrowers quickly, and returns with a maintainable filter. The Clean Air Library would test those operating details at two pickup sites before proposing a larger neighborhood fleet.

The trial design would specify ten non-ozone-generating HEPA units, room-volume and clean-air-delivery-rate matching, numbered filters, two permissioned pickup hubs, multilingual setup cards, and a documented cleaning and replacement protocol. A checkout drill would happen before smoke season. During a qualifying air-quality event, consenting participants could compare indoor and outdoor PM2.5 without providing diagnoses or other medical data; the project would not promise treatment or replace public-health guidance.

Continue evidence would require all available units to leave a hub within two hours, complete custody and filter records, correct room matching and setup for at least eight of ten borrowers, and a measurable indoor PM2.5 reduction where monitoring is accepted. Acquisition should be considered only after host capacity, likely borrowers, room requirements, maintenance responsibility, storage, and deployment timing are validated. Poor setup, weak maintenance ownership, or unsafe equipment would revise or stop the proposal.$description$,
  updated_at = '2026-08-10 13:25:00+00'
where id = '00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000201'
  and slug = 'clean-air-library';
